```dart
@override
SqliteDatabase openReadOnly(String path) {
  try {
    return (openOverride ?? _openBundledLibrary)(path);
  } on ArgumentError catch (error) {
    if (!isSqliteDynamicLibraryLoadFailure(error)) rethrow;
    // gh-1574 r2: successor-side layout repair — a pre-fix installer (or
    // one `fa update` run by a pre-fix binary) leaves the bundled libs in
    // the inert <exe_dir>/lib layout. Move libsqlite3.* into the
    // <exe_dir>/../lib the resolver reads and retry the load once; the
    // first read on an updated binary thus heals the install.
    final repaired = (layoutRepair ?? repairStaleSqliteBundleLayout)();
    if (repaired) {
      try {
        return (openOverride ?? _openBundledLibrary)(path);
      } on ArgumentError catch (retryError) {
        if (!isSqliteDynamicLibraryLoadFailure(retryError)) rethrow;
      }
    }
    throw StateError(sqliteUnavailableMessage(path));
  }
```

Careful with `rethrow` inside nested catch — rethrow inside the inner catch rethrows the inner error, fine (we're still within the outer catch's dynamic scope... actually rethrow inner catch works — it rethrows the inner exception to the caller of openReadOnly, since the outer catch has already caught the outer error; the rethrow propagates OUT of the whole openReadOnly. That's the desired semantics: non-load retry errors (e.g. SqliteException "not a database" on retry) pass through untouched.

